.\" Revised Draft prepared from current source; see EDITORS-NOTES.md.
.\" Source: src/main.c, src/radio.c, src/modes.c, service/
.TH "RADIOD" "8" "October 23, 2026" "ka9q-radio" "ka9q-radio Manual"
.SH NAME
radiod \- serve multiple SDR receiver channels over IP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B radiod
.RI "[options] [--] configuration"
.SH DESCRIPTION
radiod reads real or complex samples from one SDR frontend, performs a shared
forward FFT, and runs independently tuned receiver channels. Channels provide
linear, FM, broadcast-stereo, IQ, or spectrum processing. Audio and IQ are
sent over RTP; control and status use ka9q-radio's TLV protocol.
.PP
The program runs in the foreground. A service manager such as systemd normally
starts and supervises it. Multiple instances can run, subject to device and
host resources. Select a distinct device and control identity for each
instance.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B "\-N name"
Set the instance name. By default the configuration argument is used.
.TP
.B "\-sserial"
Select a frontend by serial number, where supported. The optional argument
must immediately follow -s, with no intervening space.
.TP
.B "\-bbus"
Select a USB bus, where supported. The optional argument must immediately
follow -b.
.TP
.B "\-ddevice"
Select a USB device number, where supported. The optional argument must
immediately follow -d. USB bus/device numbers can change after reconnecting.
.TP
.B "\-v"
Increase diagnostic verbosity. May be repeated. Verbose operation reports FFT
timings and process CPU usage periodically.
.TP
.B "\-V"
Print version information and exit. Version information is also printed at
startup.
.TP
.B "\-\-"
End option processing. Useful before the configuration pathname.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration is an INI file or a directory of files ending in .conf.
Directory fragments are concatenated in filename order. If the supplied path
is an existing regular file, only that file is read. If it does not exist,
radiod also tries the pathname with .d appended.
.PP
The [global] section selects the hardware section and the control/status
destination. A frontend section names the driver and device settings.
Additional sections define static receiver channels; a template supplies
defaults for dynamically created channels.
.PP
blocktime is in seconds: the default 0.02 means 20 ms of new samples. With
overlap = 5, each FFT includes approximately 5 ms of history, making a 25 ms
transform. Default multicast TTL 0 keeps output on the local host; configure
LAN operation explicitly.
.PP
Presets supply groups of parameters; explicit settings can override them. A
preset name is not an independent persistent demodulation mode. Consult the
configuration reference for precedence and driver-specific parameters.
.SH SIGNALS
SIGTERM requests shutdown and a successful exit. SIGINT and SIGQUIT also stop
the process. SIGUSR1 increases verbosity; SIGUSR2 decreases it. These signals
do not reload configuration.
.SH FILES
/etc/radio/radiod@NAME.conf and /etc/radio/radiod@NAME.conf.d are conventional
instance configurations. Presets and driver modules are installed under the
build's share/ka9q-radio and lib/ka9q-radio directories. Local source
installations normally use /usr/local; distribution packages normally use
/usr.
.PP
/var/lib/ka9q-radio holds application state, including FFTW wisdom.
/etc/radio/devices holds local device configuration overrides for udev
autostart. /etc/radio/enable-udev-autostart enables that startup mechanism
when the supplied rules and helper are installed.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
LANG selects the locale. A locale with digit grouping, such as en_US.UTF-8, is
recommended for the interactive frequency display.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a configuration directly, or manage a manually configured systemd
instance:
.PP
.nf
radiod \-v /etc/radio/radiod@hf.conf
sudo systemctl start radiod@hf
journalctl \-u radiod@hf \-f
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates normal termination by SIGTERM or completion of -V. Startup and
configuration errors return nonzero status. SIGINT and SIGQUIT currently
return a software-error status.
.SH SEE ALSO
control(1), tune(1), monitor(1), pcmrecord(1), fft-gen(1), systemctl(1)
.PP
Project documentation: https://github.com/ka9q/ka9q-radio
